vmodem - a modem for DOS that dials over TCP
============================================

This plugin gives a DOS serial port a Hayes modem whose "phone line" is
a TCP socket. DOS dials with ATD, and the far end is a host and port.

The AT command set, the telnet option negotiation and the socket are in
libmodemu2k <https://github.com/theimpossibleastronaut/modemu2k>. This
plugin connects that library to dosemu2's emulated 16550.


Requirements
------------

libmodemu2k 0.2.5 or newer, with its development headers. `configure`
lists `modemu` among the enabled plugins when it is found.

0.2.5 is not released yet, so build it from git master for now. The
driver needs m2k_pending_to_line(), which is the port's transmit-queue
depth and was added after 0.2.4.


Configuration
-------------

In ~/.dosemurc:

    $_com1 = "vmodem"


Test 1: dial out, using only DOS built-ins
------------------------------------------

This needs nothing installed in the DOS drive.

In a Linux terminal, listen on a port:

    nc -l 2323

Start dosemu2 with the config above, and at the DOS prompt:

    ECHO ATD"127.0.0.1 2323" > COM1
    ECHO HELLO FROM DOS > COM1

`nc` prints HELLO FROM DOS. That is the DOS-to-peer direction.

For the other direction, read the port from DOS:

    TYPE COM1

It prints CONNECT, the two lines about escaping to command mode, and
then whatever you type into the `nc` window. `TYPE` stops at a Ctrl-Z,
so to get back to the DOS prompt, type Ctrl-V Ctrl-Z in the `nc`
window and press Enter. (Ctrl-V tells the terminal to pass the next
key through instead of acting on it.)

`ECHO` cannot hang up. The call is online at this point, so anything
written to COM1 goes down the socket, `ATH` included. Quit dosemu2, or
use a terminal program as below. Test 2 is the one that shows the
whole call.


Test 2: dial out from a DOS terminal program
--------------------------------------------

Any DOS comm program works - Terminate, Telix, Telemate, or the
terminal built into Dos Navigator. Set it to COM1. The line speed does
not matter: nothing on the wire runs at it.

Dial the same listener as above:

    ATD"127.0.0.1 2323"

Anything typed in either window appears in the other.

To reach a real telnet BBS, dial its host and port:

    ATD"bbs.example.org 23"

`+++` returns to command mode without dropping the call, `ATO` goes
back online, and `ATH` hangs up. Leave about a second of silence
before and after the `+++`, as with a real modem: three plus signs in
the middle of a stream are data, not an escape.


Seeing what the modem is doing
------------------------------

Run dosemu2 with `-D+s` to log the serial subsystem. The library's own
narration appears there, prefixed MODEMU2K.
